+# Authenticated Google Chrome Session
+
+Use this workflow when the user has an authenticated page open in Google Chrome
+and Safari automation is unavailable or the site requires Chrome-specific
+extensions, signing software, or browser behavior.
+
+Core rule: let Chrome retain the authenticated session. Never read, export,
+print, copy, or persist cookies, `cookieStore`, `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`,
+authorization headers, access tokens, or refresh tokens. Persist only bounded
+page content, URLs after sensitive query values are redacted, downloaded files,
+and sanitized task evidence.
+
+## Enable Apple Events JavaScript
+
+Chrome rejects `execute ... javascript` until the user manually enables:
+
+```text
+View -> Developer -> Allow JavaScript from Apple Events
+```
+
+The menu item is a Chrome preference, not a per-tab permission. If AppleScript
+reports that JavaScript through Apple Events is turned off, ask the user to
+enable this exact item. Do not use UI scripting to toggle it.
+
+## Choose Silent Or Visible Mode
+
+Use **silent mode** by default. Inspect and interact with the exact Chrome tab
+through Apple Events without activating Chrome, raising a window, or selecting
+the tab. This is the normal mode when the user asks the agent to investigate or
+operate on a page but does not need to watch or take over.
+
+Use **visible/handoff mode** only when the user explicitly asks to open or show
+the page, work in the tab they are currently using, bring the relevant tab
+forward, or leave the browser ready for their manual interaction. In that mode,
+focus only the exact resolved window and tab needed for the request. Do not
+interpret permission to work in an authenticated tab as permission to surface
+Chrome; the visible transition must be part of the user's request.
+
+If a step unexpectedly needs human interaction, such as a CAPTCHA, passkey,
+OTP, signing prompt, file picker, or permission dialog, remain in silent mode,
+describe the exact required action, and let the user foreground the browser
+unless they explicitly ask the agent to do it.
+
+## Resolve Exact Targets Without Focus
+
+Do not use `activate`, `set index of window`, System Events clicks, or frontmost
+window assumptions. Enumerate Chrome windows and tabs without changing focus:
+
+```applescript
+tell application "Google Chrome"
+  set output to ""
+  repeat with chromeWindow in windows
+    set output to output & "window=" & (id of chromeWindow as text) & linefeed
+    repeat with chromeTab in tabs of chromeWindow
+      set output to output & "  tab=" & (id of chromeTab as text) & ¬
+        " title=" & (title of chromeTab as text) & ¬
+        " url=" & (URL of chromeTab as text) & linefeed
+    end repeat
+  end repeat
+  return output
+end tell
+```
+
+Resolve the target from a sanitized enumeration and then pin every later action
+to both IDs. Do not keep using an old ID after tabs or windows have changed.
+Never guess from tab position or operate on `active tab` merely because it was
+correct during an earlier step.
+
+The verified silent-mode pattern is to resolve `targetWindow` and `targetTab`, then
+run every DOM read or same-page interaction directly with `execute targetTab
+javascript ...`. This does not require Chrome to become active, the window to be
+raised, or the tab to be selected. DOM `click()` calls and dispatched input,
+pointer, or mouse events stay in page context and are background-safe under the
+same rule. Do not wrap this pattern with `activate`, `set active tab`, `set index
+of window`, `bring to front`, or System Events UI scripting; those additions are
+what create focus churn and belong only in an explicitly requested visible
+handoff.
+
+Treat user-owned tabs as borrowed state: do not close, reload, navigate, or move
+them unless the user explicitly requests that action. Track and close only tabs
+or windows created by the agent.
+
+## Verify DOM Access
+
+Run a bounded, non-mutating smoke test against the exact IDs:
+
+```applescript
+tell application "Google Chrome"
+  set targetWindow to first window whose id is 123456
+  set targetTab to first tab of targetWindow whose id is 123457
+  return execute targetTab javascript ¬
+    "JSON.stringify({title:document.title,origin:location.origin,readyState:document.readyState,bodyPresent:!!document.body})"
+end tell
+```
+
+This confirms that the toggle is enabled and that the intended tab is reachable.
+Before a mutation, re-check `location.origin` and a page-specific marker so a
+stale tab ID cannot send a click to the wrong page.
+
+## Read And Interact Safely
+
+- Keep DOM reads bounded with `slice(...)` and bounded element lists.
+- Prefer semantic selectors, accessible names, stable labels, and explicit form
+  values. Avoid screen-coordinate clicks.
+- Inspect current state before each consequential click. Verify the resulting
+  state afterward.
+- Stop for passkeys, OTPs, CAPTCHAs, signing confirmations, file pickers, or
+  permission prompts that require human interaction. Tell the user exactly what
+  must be confirmed without focusing Chrome for them.
+- Do not submit payments, legal filings, signatures, or other consequential
+  actions unless the user has explicitly authorized that mutation.
+- Do not transfer authenticated browser requests to `curl` by copying session
+  material. Use same-origin page-context requests only when needed and return
+  only bounded, sanitized response data.
+
+Apple Events output may contain personal data. Write raw command output only to
+a task-scoped private file (`umask 077`), sanitize it with
+`mac-document-sanitize`, and inspect only the returned sanitized path. Remove
+only raw artifacts created by the agent after the task no longer needs them.
+
+Chrome AppleScript is currently a documented low-level workflow, not a guarded
+`mac-chrome-session` CLI. Apply the safety checks in this reference manually.
